.TH PHASORSTD_RAND 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_rand \- Phasor pseudo-random and cryptographically-secure random number generation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBinclude "std/rand";\fR
.PP
.B rand_seed(s1, s2)
.B rand_next_range(min, max)
.B rand_next_float()
.B rand_next_float_range(min, max)
.B rand_crypto_int()
.B rand_crypto_float()
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This module provides two independent sources of randomness:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A fast, seedable xorshift+ pseudo-random generator
.RB ( rand_seed() ", " rand_next_range() ", " rand_next_float() ", " rand_next_float_range() ),
suitable for simulations, games, and anywhere reproducibility is wanted. This generator's
state is global to the process (not per-VM) and is
.B not
safe to use concurrently from multiple threads without external synchronization.
.IP \(bu 2
An OS-backed cryptographically-secure generator
.RB ( rand_crypto_int() ", " rand_crypto_float() ),
suitable for tokens, keys, and other security-sensitive values. It cannot be seeded and does
not use the xorshift+ state at all.
.RE
.SH PSEUDO-RANDOM FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR rand_seed (s1, s2)
Seed the pseudo-random number generator with two positive integer values.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBs1\fR 12
First seed value; must be a positive non-zero integer
.IP \fBs2\fR 12
Second seed value; must be a positive non-zero integer
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Null value
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not an integer, or if either seed is not strictly
positive
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR rand_next_range (min, max)
Generate a pseudo-random integer in the closed interval [\fBmin\fR, \fBmax\fR].
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBmin\fR 12
Lower bound of the range (inclusive)
.IP \fBmax\fR 12
Upper bound of the range (inclusive)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer in the range [\fBmin\fR, \fBmax\fR]
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not an integer, or if \fBmin\fR is greater than \fBmax\fR
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR rand_next_float ()
Generate a pseudo-random double-precision float.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
Float in the range [0.0, 1.0)
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR rand_next_float_range (min, max)
Generate a pseudo-random double-precision float in a given range.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBmin\fR 12
Lower bound of the range (inclusive); integer or float
.IP \fBmax\fR 12
Upper bound of the range (exclusive); integer or float
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Float in the range [\fBmin\fR, \fBmax\fR)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not a number, or if \fBmin\fR is greater than \fBmax\fR
.RE
.SH CRYPTOGRAPHIC FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR rand_crypto_int ()
Generate a cryptographically-secure random 64-bit integer, sourced from the operating
system's CSPRNG (\fBBCryptGenRandom\fR on Windows, \fBSecRandomCopyBytes\fR on Apple
platforms, \fBgetrandom()\fR on Linux, or \fB/dev/urandom\fR elsewhere).
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
A random 64-bit integer covering the full \fBi64\fR range (positive or negative), or null if
the underlying OS randomness source could not be read
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR rand_crypto_float ()
Generate a cryptographically-secure random double-precision float.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
Float in the range [0.0, 1.0), built from 53 random bits, or null if the underlying OS
randomness source could not be read
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Basic Usage
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/rand";

// Seed the generator
rand_seed(123456, 789012);

// Generate a random integer between 1 and 100
var n: int = rand_next_range(1, 100);
putf("Random number: %d", n);

// Generate a random float
var d: float = rand_next_float();
putf("Random float: %f", d);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Reproducible Sequences
.PP
.nf
.RS
// Same seeds always produce the same sequence
rand_seed(42, 1337);
var a: int = rand_next_range(0, 9);
var b: int = rand_next_range(0, 9);

rand_seed(42, 1337);
var c: int = rand_next_range(0, 9);
var d: int = rand_next_range(0, 9);

// a == c and b == d
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B A Float in a Custom Range
.PP
.nf
.RS
rand_seed(111, 222);

// Random float between -5.0 and 5.0
var scaled: float = rand_next_float_range(-5.0, 5.0);
putf("Scaled value: %f", scaled);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Generating a Secure RNG
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/rand";
include "std/type";

var token: int = rand_crypto_int();
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The pseudo-random generator's state is global and starts at (0, 0) until
.BR rand_seed()
is called; with an all-zero xorshift+ state every subsequent call to
.BR rand_next_range() ", " rand_next_float() ", or " rand_next_float_range()
will deterministically return the same degenerate value (effectively zero) until the
generator has been seeded at least once
.IP \(bu 2
.B rand_seed()
throws if either seed is zero or negative; there is no silent coercion of invalid arguments
.IP \(bu 2
Using the same pair of seeds always reproduces the same sequence of pseudo-random values
.IP \(bu 2
.BR rand_next_range() " is inclusive of both " min " and " max ;
.B rand_next_float_range()
is inclusive of \fBmin\fR but exclusive of \fBmax\fR
.IP \(bu 2
The pseudo-random generator is not cryptographically secure and must not be used for
security-sensitive values (tokens, keys, passwords); use
.BR rand_crypto_int() " or " rand_crypto_float()
for those instead
.IP \(bu 2
.BR rand_crypto_int() " and " rand_crypto_float()
do not require or use
.BR rand_seed() ;
they always draw fresh entropy from the operating system and can return null if that source
is unavailable
